finding.description:
Retail accepts a scenery candidate only if all of these hold:
(a) the point is inside [0,192);
(b) it is not on a road;
(c) its 24 m land cell does not hold a building (a building is added only to the cell containing its origin);
(d) the walkable triangle's N.z is within [min_slope, max_slope];
(e) CPhysicsObj::obj_within_block passes: no sorting sphere or cylsphere crosses the landblock edge.
Retail has NO scenery-vs-scenery rejection. holtburger's default bake differs on each point. It rejects any candidate whose render-mesh 2D AABB overlaps a building AABB, which over-rejects in neighbouring cells and under-rejects inside the building's own cell. It rejects any candidate whose AABB overlaps an already placed scenery AABB, which thins dense forests because overlapping canopies drop their later member. It never runs the slope check: 705 of 990 census rows (71%) have min_slope > 0, mostly 0.87-0.98, so retail keeps trees off steep hillsides where holtburger plants them. It has no edge-crossing rejection, and it accepts lx == 192. The rationale ('ACE never implements it', the Scenery.cs:69 TODO) applies only to ACE's dead Entity/Scenery.cs. ACE's live physics implements every retail gate. Partly re-raised: the 2026-05-29 W1 note fixed only the Strict-mode slope units. The new evidence is that ACE's live server uses these gates, so the default is wrong for server agreement too. Strict mode's slope check also uses a bilinear 4-corner gradient (height.rs:346-375) instead of the walkable triangle normal.

