finding.description:
Per run, holtburger's measurement is already more rigorous than WebAC's:
- moving-bench drives the camera from a frame-indexed pose table, pins the anchor, checksums the realised path and rejects churn instead of averaging it;
- flag-bench interleaves fresh-profile arms on the 1070;
- hb-results-v2 requires @scale tags and a controlSpread.
But every comparison is an A/B someone asked for. Nothing stores results or compares against history, so a default-on change that adds draws, triangles or programs at Holtburg is caught only if someone happens to bench it. moving-bench also writes `wasmProfile: "unknown"`, although the dev-wasm build carries a known ~4× tax, and it reports no program count. WebAC's ratchet stores every run and compares it against the median of the last 3 comparable runs. Runs are comparable only with the same renderer string, location (same landblock, within 50 m), phase length, sampling mode and draw distance. A run whose own verdict was 'regression' never becomes a baseline, so one regressed run cannot bless the next. A value inside the baselines' recorded spread counts as noise. On a software renderer only scene-cost counts are compared; fps and hitches compare only between real-GPU runs. Those rules came from concrete false alarms (same-code runs moved geometries +32% and renderJs +72%).
